cc:  
Subject: Re: FERC Data Warehouse


FERC Background:
When Sensecorp was hired to install the ACTA Rapidmarts, Enron requested a 
FERC Rapidmart which had not yet been defined by ACTA.  In order to fulfill 
Enron's requirements, Sensecorp hired a FERC expert, Sean O'Reilly to assist 
us in defining and developing a FERC Rapidmart.  At the time that Sean 
structured the Rapidmart he stated that the FERC information did not appear 
to reconcile with FI appropriately and that some changes in SAP were probably 
required to ensure that the information was correct.  Also before Sean left, 
he demonstrated to some Enron business representatives the functionality that 
would be available in the FERC Rapidmart but that the information would still 
need to be investigated and appropriately reviewed.

Project Sunrise:
At the time that Project Sunrise was initiated and there appeared to be some 
downtime for the Sensecorp developers, I approached Beverly Fennessy to 
contact FERC business representatives to review the FERC information and 
determine if it could be used with a few minor changes.  ( I was hoping that 
we might be able to quickly complete something that might be of use to the 
business)  We then set up a meeting with the FERC representatives to review 
the data.  

FERC Review:
However, as myself and Scott Brudner reviewed the information, we discovered 
that we are not FERC experts, and all the value that we would be able to 
provide in a meeting is to demonstrate the tables that were selected and the 
structure of the data.   We would not be able to explain FERC, how the module 
worked, or what the information meant.   It seemed that there would not be 
any true benefit in realizing a meeting to review that information - 
especially since we were aware that problems existed in the SAP FERC module 
which we would not be able to explain.  We felt that the only way to have a 
truly productive meeting and progress in the development of FERC was to have 
Sean O'Reilly return and assist us.  However, since Project Sunrise is still 
in the business requirements gathering phase, Beverly thought it was best 
(and I agreed) not to charge further development expenses to the project 
until we were in that phase of the project.  We thought it was best to 
investigate and further develop FERC once the project scope had been defined 
and FERC was included as a priority.   At that point in time we decided to 
cancel the meeting and informed the appropriate parties. 

Business Interest:
As you can tell from the emails below, there is renewed interest in reviewing 
the FERC information and furthering development.  However, Scott and myself 
will not be able to demonstrate FERC functionality to the business anymore 
than was already demonstrated to them by Sean in August.  In order to provide 
true value, we require the assistance of Sean and/or a tremendous amount of 
time to become FERC experts ourselves.  Neither of these items are feasible 
by tomorrow morning.  Please advise how we should proceed.  


Thank you,

Tzlem
