he principal energy suppliers, most of them headquartered outside the state=
. The companies had insisted on being released from the state's legal claim=
s as part of any settlement, according to sources close to the negotiations=
.  "We will have a viable claim in state court and other jurisdictions for =
the remainder," Kahn said. " California will get its $8.9 billion."  But th=
e state's own expert witness, economist Eric Hildebrandt, chief market moni=
tor for the California grid, testified before Wagner that the out-of-state =
